On his <a href="https://podcasts.apple.com/us/podcast/apple-wants-to-take-over-your-home-is-siri-finally-ready/id6817744297?i=1000792537082">first-ever <em>Power On</em> podcast</a> today, <em>Bloomberg</em>'s Mark Gurman said that he believes Apple will introduce its long-rumored 14-inch and 16-inch MacBook Pro models with touch-enabled OLED displays in October or November this year.<br/>
<br/>
<img src="https://images.macrumors.com/article-new/2026/04/MacBook-Pro-Low-Angle-Wide-Lens.jpg" alt="" width="2500" height="1406" class="aligncenter size-full wp-image-1046019" /><br/>
"Apple, I believe, will introduce its first touchscreen MacBook Pro this fall, as early as October," he said. "It could slip to November, but as early as October."<br/>
<br/>
Apple is testing the laptops with macOS 27.2, which is currently in beta, he added. The software update is expected to be released towards the end of October, so the touch-screen MacBook Pro models might be announced later this month.<br/>
<br/>
Other rumored features beyond an OLED touch screen include Apple's existing M5 Pro and M5 Max chips, a thinner design, and a Dynamic Island instead of a notch.<br/>
<br/>
A few years ago, Gurman reported that Apple was <a href="https://www.macrumors.com/2024/12/06/apple-cellular-mac-custom-modem/">looking into the idea of cellular Macs</a> that would be able to connect to a 5G or LTE network without relying on an iPhone's hotspot. For this reason, some Apple fans are hopeful that the touch-screen MacBook Pro models will be equipped with the company's custom-designed C1X or C2 modem.<br/>
<br/>
